ITEMS=[
("A house is owned outright, with no private lease or other private encumbrance identified; only governmental limitations remain. Which interest is being valued?",
 ["Fee simple interest","Leased fee interest","Life estate interest","Remainder interest"],
 "The fee simple interest is the full ownership estate subject only to governmental limitations. A leased fee would require a lease in place."),
("An owner rents a duplex to a tenant under a current agreement and keeps the right to the rent and to regain possession at its end. Which interest does the owner hold?",
 ["Leased fee interest","Leasehold interest","Fee simple interest","Sandwich leasehold interest"],
 "The landlord holds the leased fee; the tenant holds the leasehold. Fee simple would mean no lease encumbers the property."),
("A tenant holds the contractual right to occupy a dwelling for five more years. Which property interest is being analyzed for that tenant?",
 ["Leasehold interest","Leased fee interest","Life estate interest","Tenancy at will"],
 "The tenant's possessory rights under a lease for a stated term constitute a leasehold interest. A tenancy at will has no fixed term."),
("A deed grants possession for the lifetime of a named individual, after which possession passes to another person. Which present interest is described?",
 ["Life estate interest","Remainder interest","Leasehold interest","Fee simple interest"],
 "A life estate lasts for a specified person's lifetime. The person who takes possession afterward holds the remainder, a future interest."),
("A deed begins at a monument and follows bearings and measured distances around a parcel back to the beginning. Which description method is used?",
 ["Metes and bounds","Rectangular survey system","Lot and block (recorded plat)","Street address"],
 "Metes and bounds follows the parcel's boundary courses and distances from a point of beginning."),
("A deed identifies the parcel by its number within a subdivision map filed with the county. Which description method is used?",
 ["Lot and block (recorded plat)","Metes and bounds","Rectangular survey system","Assessor's parcel number"],
 "A recorded plat or lot-and-block reference identifies the parcel within a filed subdivision map. An assessor's parcel number is a tax identifier, not a legal description."),
("A deed identifies land by a fraction of a section within a specified township and range. Which description method is used?",
 ["Rectangular survey system","Lot and block (recorded plat)","Metes and bounds","State plane coordinates"],
 "The rectangular (government) survey system uses principal meridians, baselines, townships, ranges and sections."),
("A delivery record identifies a property only as 18 Willow Lane, without boundary courses, a plat reference or a survey-system reference. What identification has it supplied?",
 ["An address, not a legal description","A metes-and-bounds description","A lot-and-block description","A rectangular survey description"],
 "A street address helps locate a property, but it is not by itself a legal description."),
("A site report measures the length of the parcel boundary adjoining its access street. Which site characteristic is measured?",
 ["Frontage","Depth","Perimeter","Access"],
 "Frontage is the extent of the site along the street. Access concerns how the site can be reached, not a measured length."),
("A rectangular lot extends 140 feet from its street boundary to its rear boundary. Which dimension is described?",
 ["Depth","Frontage","Width","Setback"],
 "Depth measures the front-to-rear extent, rather than the street-facing length. A setback is a required distance from a boundary to a building."),
("Contour mapping shows that a site rises sharply toward the rear. Which site characteristic does this describe?",
 ["Topography","Drainage","Soil bearing capacity","Orientation"],
 "Topography describes the land's surface relief, slope and contour. Slope can affect drainage, but the mapping describes the land's shape."),
("After rainfall, water accumulates in a low corner and leaves the site slowly. Which site characteristic should be investigated directly?",
 ["Drainage","Topography","Flood zone designation","Orientation"],
 "Drainage concerns the movement and removal of water; slope can affect it but is not the whole issue. A flood zone designation concerns mapped flood risk, not on-site runoff."),
("A city ordinance limits a parcel to residential use. Which source of limitation applies?",
 ["Zoning","A deed restriction","A building code","An easement"],
 "A municipal ordinance limiting use is zoning, a public land-use restriction. A building code governs construction standards rather than permitted uses."),
("A recorded covenant imposed by an earlier owner prohibits detached workshops, although zoning permits them. Which source of limitation applies?",
 ["A private deed restriction","A zoning variance","A nonconforming use","An easement in gross"],
 "A covenant imposed through private title arrangements is a private restriction, distinct from zoning, and can be stricter than zoning."),
("A recorded right allows a neighbor to cross a defined strip to reach a public road without owning the strip. What interest is described?",
 ["An access easement","A license","An encroachment","A right of first refusal"],
 "An easement permits a specified use of another parcel without transferring ownership. A license is a revocable personal permission and is not normally a recorded interest."),
("A survey shows that a garage wall physically extends two feet over the lot line without an identified right to occupy that area. What issue is identified?",
 ["An encroachment","An easement","An adverse possession claim","A setback violation"],
 "A physical intrusion across a property boundary is an encroachment; its legal consequences, including any adverse possession claim, require separate investigation."),
("For a geometry exercise, a rectangular footprint measures 30 feet by 40 feet, with no offsets. What is its area?",
 ["1,200 square feet","1,400 square feet","140 square feet","2,400 square feet"],
 "Area is length x width: 30 x 40 = 1,200 square feet. 140 feet is the perimeter, a length rather than an area."),
("An L-shaped footprint consists of a 40-by-40-foot rectangle with a 10-by-10-foot corner cut out. What area remains?",
 ["1,500 square feet","1,600 square feet","1,400 square feet","1,300 square feet"],
 "The area is 1,600 - 100 = 1,500 square feet."),
("A triangular parcel has a base of 60 feet and a perpendicular height of 60 feet. What is its area?",
 ["1,800 square feet","3,600 square feet","1,200 square feet","900 square feet"],
 "Triangle area is one-half x base x height: 0.5 x 60 x 60 = 1,800 square feet. 3,600 forgets the one-half."),
("Two nonoverlapping rectangular wings measure 30 by 50 feet and 20 by 30 feet. What is their combined footprint area?",
 ["2,100 square feet","2,000 square feet","1,800 square feet","3,000 square feet"],
 "The areas are 1,500 and 600 square feet, totaling 2,100 square feet."),
]
